# Explore the Thermodynamics of Sorting

A shuffled collection of $n$ distinct items has $n!$ possible orderings. This notebook-like tour follows that one factorial through decision complexity, logical erasure, and reversible memory. For background, see [sorting algorithms](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sorting_algorithm), [Landauer's principle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landauer%27s_principle), and [reversible computing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reversible_computing).

## 1. Count the possibilities

Begin with the live factorial resource explorer. It calculates $\log_2(n!)$, its integer ceiling, and the minimum number of history states.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the state-counting argument</summary>
A binary tree of height $h$ has at most $2^h$ leaves. Distinguishing $n!$ permutations requires $n!\le 2^h$, hence $h\ge\lceil\log_2(n!)ceil$. A constant visible sorting map has a one-state image, so it erases $\log_2(n!)$ bits.
</details>

{{visualization:0}}

## 2. Turn erased bits into a work scale

At temperature $T$, multiply erased bits by $kT\ln2$. The bases cancel, yielding $kT\ln(n!)$.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:0}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal assumptions behind the energy statement</summary>
The displayed value is a logical Landauer scale for a many-to-one map. It is not the measured energy of a particular processor. Device losses, finite-time operation, control, and noise lie outside the state-counting model.
</details>

## 3. Keep history and reverse the computation

A reversible sorter can output the visible sorted state together with the original permutation. The complete map is then bijective and erases zero information. Since the visible output is fixed, histories for distinct inputs must differ, requiring at least $n!$ states.

{{demo:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the history lower-bound proof</summary>
If two input permutations produced the same auxiliary history, their visible components would also agree. Their complete output pairs would coincide, contradicting reversibility. Thus input permutations inject into history states.
</details>

## 4. Break the comparison-equals-heat intuition

Duplicate a tree beneath a redundant binary branch. Repeat this operation $r$ times. Height rises by $r$, but the visible function does not change.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the padding proof</summary>
Each new branch adds one to height and duplicates the leaves. Therefore transcript capacity cannot decrease. Since logical erasure depends on the domain and image of the visible map rather than tree height, the Landauer scale is invariant.
</details>

## 5. Synthesis

The same $n!$ governs three separate resources: binary decision depth, visible information loss, and reversible history capacity. The mathematics does not assign one erased bit to every comparison. To make that physical claim one must model comparison records and their reset protocol explicitly.

